). (5,4) has sum 9 >6, not allowed. So line y = x-1 passes through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) but (4,3) is not in set because (4,3) sum 7 >6, not allowed. Actually (4,3) sum =7 >6, so not allowed. So line y = x-1 only covers (2,1),(3,2). That's 2 points.\n\nLine y = x-2 passes through (3,1),(4,2),(5,3) but (5,3) sum=8 >6, not allowed. So only (3,1),(4,2). That's 2 points.\n\nLine y = x-3 passes through (4,1),(5,2) but (5,2) sum=7 >6, not allowed. So only (4,1). That's 1 point.\n\nLine y = x-4 passes through (5,1) only.\n\nSo we could use lines of slope 1 to cover some points.\n\nAlternatively, use lines of slope -2 maybe.\n\nBut we need to ensure each line is not horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. So slopes like 1, 2, -2, 1/2, etc. are allowed.\n\nWe need to cover all points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). That's 10 points.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So each line can cover at most 4 points (since there are columns 2,3,4,5). So total capacity is 4*4=16 >10, so capacity is fine.\n\nWe need to assign points to lines such that each line is straight. Let's try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points.\n\nOne approach: Use line L1: through (2,4) and (5,1). Slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So can't use that.\n\nTry L1: through (2,4) and (4,2). Slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nTry L1: through (2,4) and (3,2). Slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2/1 = -2, allowed. Equation: y-4 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x + 8. Check other points: (4,0) not in set; (5,-2) not in set. So L1 covers (2,4) and (3,2). Also maybe (4,? )? For x=4, y = -2*4+8 = 0, not in set. So L1 covers those two.\n\nL2: through (2,3) and (5,1). Slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, allowed. Equation: y-3 = -2/3 (x-2) => y = -2/3 x + (something). For x=5, y = -10/3 + something = 1 => something = 1 + 10/3 = 13/3, so equation y = -2/3 x + 13/3. Check integer points: For x=3, y = -2 + 13/3 = 7/3, not integer. So L2 only covers (2,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\nL3: through (2,2) and (4,1). Slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, allowed. Equation: y-2 = -1/2 (x-2) => y = -1/2 x + 3. For x=3, y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5, not integer. So L3 covers (2,2) and (4,1). Good.\n\nL4: through (2,1) and (3,1) maybe? That's horizontal (slope 0), not allowed. So need a different line for (2,1),(3,1),(4,? ),(5,? ). Could use line through (2,1) and (5,1) horizontal, not allowed. So we need to cover (2,1),(3,1),(4,? ), maybe (3,1) and (4,2) slope (2-1)/(4-3)=1, allowed. But (4,2) is already covered by L3? Actually L3 covers (4,1), not (4,2). So (4,2) is still uncovered. Let's try L4: through (3,1) and (4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-3)=1, allowed. Equation: y-1 = 1*(x-3) => y = x - 2. For x=2, y=0, not in set. For x=5, y=3, not in set (5,3) not allowed. So L4 covers (3,1) and (4,2). Good.\n\nNow we have covered points: L1: (2,4),(3,2); L2: (2,3),(5,1); L3: (2,2),(4,1); L4: (3,1),(4,2). Uncovered points: (2,1) and (3,? ) maybe (3,3) and (3,? ) Actually we haven't covered (3,3) and (2,1). Also (3,? ) we have (3,3) not covered. (2,1) not covered. (3,? ) we have (3,? ) we covered (3,2) and (3,1) but not (3,3). (4,? ) we covered (4,1) and (4,2) but not (4,? ) there is no (4,3) because sum >6. So we have uncovered (2,1) and (3,3). That's 2 points left, but we have used all 4 sunny lines. We have a vertical line x=1 covering column 1, but we still need to cover (2,1) and (3,3). We have no lines left. So this assignment fails.\n\nWe could try a different assignment. Perhaps we need to use the vertical line not at x=1 but at some other column to cover more points. For n=5, we could use vertical line x=2 to cover all points in column 2 (4 points). Then we need to cover remaining points with 4 sunny lines (since total lines =5, k=4). But we have only 4 sunny lines left, but we used vertical line as non-sunny, so we have 4 sunny lines. The remaining points are all points except those in column 2: column 1 (5 points), column 3 (3 points), column 4 (2 points), column 5 (1 point). That